  cat > "$D"/'qt/ARCHITECTURE.md' <<'PILCROW_EOF_d5a6afd329'
# Pilcrow Qt client — architecture and conventions

The native desktop client: **PySide6 (Qt 6), Qt Widgets, CPU rasterised**, for machines with no
GPU where a browser engine crawls. It replaces the WebKitGTK shell on Linux (and can run on Windows
and macOS). It talks to the unchanged Node server over the same WebSocket JSON-RPC the web interface
uses, so `server/` is never touched and the web interface stays for `pilcrow --server` over SSH.

Everything the web interface does, this client does, with the same information architecture, the
same words, the same tokens (colours, type scale, radii) and the same feel. Read the React source
in `web/src` for the behaviour of anything you build: it is the specification.

```
qt/
  pilcrow_qt/
    __main__.py        python -m pilcrow_qt [--url http://127.0.0.1:PORT] [--dir ~/pilcrow] …
    app.py             QApplication, fonts, theme, main window, server discovery/launch
    rpc.py             WebSocket JSON-RPC client (QWebSocket), reconnect, events as signals
    store.py           app state + session actions (port of web/src/lib/store.ts)
    lib/               pure Python ports of web/src/lib (no Qt): transcript_model, units,
                       tools_meta, fmt, partial_json, ansi, fuzzy, workflow, models, shortcuts
                       (the one table of keys), folds (labels.ts: what a stretch of work is
                       called, quiet steps)
    theme.py           tokens for dark/light (port of styles/tokens.css); the palette they map onto,
                       tint() for a widget's own roles, a static QSS on palette roles
    icons.py           icon(name, color, size) from assets/icons/*.svg (Lucide, currentColor)
    fonts.py           loads assets/fonts/*.ttf (Inter, JetBrains Mono) when present
    markdown.py        Markdown → QTextDocument/HTML, code blocks highlighted with Pygments; the layout
                       QTextDocument cannot do itself (table columns, wrapped code, task lists)
    widgets/
      main_window.py   sidebar | main column | right panel; global shortcuts (App.tsx)
      sidebar.py       Sidebar.tsx
      session_view.py  SessionView.tsx: header, the helpers bar (HELPERS.md), transcript, composer, find bar
      transcript/      the real widgets the conversation still hosts: permission card, the
                       question and plan forms (bodies/ask, bodies/plan), code/diff helpers
    canvas/            the conversation itself (Transcript.tsx), as one painted viewport: see
                       DESIGN.md — scene.py (blocks, groups, Fenwick trees), view.py (the
                       QAbstractScrollArea: commit, anchor, prefetch, paint; input.py: hits, hover,
                       focus, selection, the context menu and links, a part of the view), transcript.py
                       (the session's scene, reconciled from the model), blocks/ (prose, user,
                       thinking, tool cards and their node-tree bodies, folds, islands, a helper's note: note.py), nodes.py
                       (the mini-layout bodies are built from), paint.py (documents, caches),
                       a11y.py (the viewport as an accessible list of its blocks)
      composer.py      Composer.tsx (the @ and / list: suggest.py; the queue above it: queue_tray.py)
      overview.py      Overview.tsx (control room)
      inbox.py         Inbox.tsx (+ the dispatch report card)
      dispatch/        one job across many repositories (DISPATCH.md): board.py (every job, requests
                       answered on the card), job_view.py (one row per repository on the canvas
                       engine, the review foot), composer.py (a new job, the plan as you type),
                       targets.py (the workspace scan as a picker, rows on the canvas engine),
                       common.py (states, the bar)
      panels/          changes (+ a lead's helpers, each worktree a section: helpers_changes.py), files (+editor),
                       terminal, preview, tasks, history (its timeline
                       is a list on the canvas engine: timeline_list.py), info
      dialogs/         new_session, settings (+pages; settings_repos.py is Settings → Repositories), schedule,
                       mcp_auth, palette, shortcuts, confirm
      ui/              shared bits: the painted button (button.py), labels, boxes, pills and hairlines
                       (bits.py), popover/menu, collapse, working mark, toasts, lightbox
    assets/icons/      generated by scripts/extract-icons.mjs
    assets/fonts/      generated by scripts/make-qt-fonts.py
  tests/               pytest (QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen) against test/mock/mock-server.mjs: harness.py
                       starts the mock and boots the window (Client); test_perf_budget.py runs the bench
  requirements.txt
  DESIGN.md            the canvas engine: why the conversation is painted, and how
  UX.md                the layout audit: what each screen says by its shape, and what was changed
  AUDIT.md             the adversarial review: what stalls, flickers or breaks the picture, measured
  REVIEW.md            the review for speed: algorithms, data structures and design against a CPU-only budget
  tests/stress/        the scripts behind AUDIT.md (streaming, flings, resizes, islands, HiDPI, the window)
```

## Rules

- **Python 3.9+** (Ubuntu 22.04 ships 3.10). `from __future__ import annotations`; no `match`,
  no `X | Y` at runtime. Type hints everywhere; dataclasses for records.
- **PySide6 only** (`from PySide6.QtWidgets import …`). No PyQt, no Qt Designer `.ui` files, no
  QML, no QtWebEngine. Widgets are built in code with layouts.
- **No GPU assumptions.** Nothing may need OpenGL. QGraphicsView is fine (software). Effects are
  cheap by design: flat surfaces, hairlines, no blur. Animations run on the one clock
  (`canvas/clock.Tween`, never a QPropertyAnimation / QVariantAnimation of their own) with the
  spring curves from `theme.py` (`spring_soft`, `spring_snappy`, `spring_bouncy`, `ease`);
  `Tween.go` lands at once under `Store.reduced_motion`.
- **`lib/` is pure.** Nothing in `qt/pilcrow_qt/lib` imports Qt. It ports the pure modules of
  `web/src/lib` under the same function names (`format` → `fmt`, `tools` → `tools_meta`,
  `partialJson` → `partial_json`, `transcript` → `transcript_model`, `labels` → `folds`; `units`,
  `workflow`, `fuzzy`, `ansi`, `models`; `shortcuts` is the table, its key matching is Qt's
  `QShortcut`), and the TypeScript tests in `test/unit` are ported to `qt/tests/test_lib_*.py`.
  What the web keeps there for React and the browser has a Qt home of its own: `rpc.py`,
  `store.py`, `markdown.py` (and highlighting), `theme.py` (motion), the canvas (find in the
  conversation: `Transcript.find_entries`; `units.extendTurns`, a render memo, has no counterpart:
  `canvas/transcript.py` reconciles the scene from the model itself).
- **State flows one way.** Widgets read from `Store` and call its actions; they never call `rpc`
  directly except through a Store action. `Store` emits Qt signals; widgets connect and re-render
  the part that changed. Transcript items are mutable objects with a `rev` counter, exactly like the
  web model; a widget keeps the `rev` it drew and skips when unchanged.
- **App-wide signals are bound, not connected.** `Store` and `theme.theme` outlive every widget, and
  a lambda connected to their signals keeps firing after its widget is deleted (then crashes on the
  dead C++ object). Always `from ..ui.signals import bind` and write
  `bind(store.sessions_changed, self, self._on_sessions)` — it disconnects when `self` is destroyed.
  Signals of a child widget (a button's `clicked`) may be connected directly.
- **Theme tokens, never literal colours.** `theme.tokens().text_2`, `theme.tokens().accent` …
  Widgets that paint use the tokens (`paint_box`, `Box`, `Pill`, `Btn` in `widgets/ui`); widgets
  that Qt paints take their colours from their palette: the application palette carries the theme
  (`theme.palette`), and `theme.tint(w, fg="text_3")` / `tint(w, bg="chrome")` gives one widget
  other roles from the tokens (a token name, a blend such as `"fill_2/canvas"`, or a function of
  the tokens), re-applied on every switch. Never put a colour in a stylesheet: the application
  stylesheet is static and names colours only as `palette(role)`; a per-widget stylesheet may
  carry geometry and fonts, and `palette(role)` only on the native fields, combos and scrollbars
  that `theme.repolish` polishes again after a switch. Switching theme swaps the palette, re-tints,
  repolishes those, and emits `theme.changed`; custom painters repaint on it.
- **Fonts**: `theme.font_ui(size)`, `theme.font_mono(size)`; sizes come from the type scale in
  tokens (`fs_ui=13`, `fs_body=14.5`, `mono=12.5`…). Chinese text falls back to the system CJK face
  through Qt's own substitution; do not hardcode families.
- **Icons**: `icons.icon("square-terminal", color=tokens.text_2, size=14)`; names are Lucide's
  kebab-case names. `icons.pixmap(...)` for painting.
- **Words**: copy the exact strings from the React components (titles, hints, empty states,
  button labels). Shortcuts too (App.tsx, Composer.tsx, Sidebar.tsx).
- **Accessibility**: every button has an accessible name (`setAccessibleName`) matching its title
  or tooltip; keyboard focus order follows the layout.
- **Tests**: every widget module gets a pytest test that builds it against the mock server (or
  a fake Store) and asserts on structure and text, the way `test/e2e/run.mjs` does for the web. No
  plugin: `tests/harness.py` starts the mock (`start_mock`), boots the window against it (`Client`)
  and drives the event loop until a condition holds (`Client.wait_until`, `Client.sleep`); events
  are sent with `QTest`.
  `python -m pytest qt/tests` must pass offscreen on Linux and Windows.
- **Style**: black-compatible formatting (120 columns), `ruff` clean, docstrings that say what a
  thing is for, comments that say why, no commented-out code.

## Runtime shapes

The server's messages and state are documented in `web/src/lib/types.ts`; the client keeps them as
plain `dict`s (JSON as it arrives) at the boundary and converts to dataclasses only inside `lib/`
(transcript items) and `store.py` (Runtime, SessionSummary). Method names and event names are the
strings in `server/main.js`; `rpc.call("session.send", {...})` returns a `Call` with `.then(fn)` /
`.catch(fn)`; `rpc.on("session.msgs", fn)` subscribes to an event.

## Running

```
pip install -r qt/requirements.txt
node test/mock/mock-server.mjs 5300            # or a real Pilcrow server
python -m pilcrow_qt --url http://127.0.0.1:5300/
```
`PYTHONPATH=qt` or `pip install -e qt` makes the package importable.
